          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My HbA1c came back at 7.2%. What does that mean, what target does guidance describe, and which code would type 2 diabetes be recorded under?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\n**What HbA1c measures:** The screening guidance says HbA1c reflects the share of your hemoglobin (the oxygen-carrying protein in red blood cells) that has been coated with sugar over the life of those cells. (Source: *Type 2 diabetes: who is screened, and the diagnostic thresholds*.) The same guidance notes that HbA1c can be less reliable with some conditions, such as anemia, pregnancy, recent blood transfusion, or advanced kidney disease.\n\n**The target:** The retrieved passages do not give a numeric HbA1c target, so I can't tell you whether 7.2% is within the range guidance describes. The glycemic targets document I found covers how low blood sugar is monitored and when a target may be relaxed, but the number itself did not come back. Your clinician can tell you what target applies to you and what your result means.\n\n**The code:** Type 2 diabetes is classified as **E11** in ICD-10-CM, the US coding system. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*.) This is a coding reference only. It does not mean you have been diagnosed with diabetes. A 7.2% result on its own is not a diagnosis, and I can't make one.\n\n**Next step:** Please take this result to the clinician who ordered it. Ask what the result means for you, whether it needs follow-up testing, and what target applies to you.",
